  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + z^2)/2, drawn at 0.4 times the loss, steep left-right. Without noise the steep direction overshoots past rate 0.4 and grows (diverges) past rate 0.8.",
    "Each step is minus the learning rate times (true slope + noise). Noise per setting has spread 1.5*sqrt((64-b)/(63 b)) for batch b: 1.5 at b=1, exactly 0 at 64.",
    "Both arrows are steps (rate times slope estimate), drawn 2.5 times their length on one shared scale. Purple: this mini-batch step, the way the ball moves. Dark: the full-data step, against the true slope.",
    "Diverged means the loss passed 6 or the ball left the drawn bowl; points off the bowl are drawn at its rim. A run ends after 40 steps. The trail holds the start plus up to 40 steps.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed sequence: Reset replays it from the start; a new run continues it, so its noise differs. With noise a step lowers the loss only on average."
  ]
}